{
  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "With no prior experiments, the key uncertainty is whether multimodal fusion (RNA + image) with multiprofile aggregation provides a meaningful accuracy boost over unimodal baselines, and which component choices matter most. Config_002 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, avgpool, concat) serves as a strong, moderate-cost baseline: it tests the core multimodal hypothesis with a learned RNA encoder (scvi_n200), a standard image encoder (resnet50), a simple aggregation (avgpool), and the simplest fusion (concat). This is not the most expensive configuration (no transformer aggregation, no ViT-L), so it preserves GPU budget for follow-up experiments that can isolate which axis (RNA encoder, image encoder, aggregation, fusion) drives performance. The result will anchor the search: if accuracy is strong, we exploit by upgrading individual components (e.g., transformer aggregation or ViT-L); if weak, we pivot to unimodal baselines or simpler encoders. Expected relative cost: moderate—multiprofile processing and a learned RNA encoder add cost, but avgpool and resnet50 keep it well below the most expensive configs (ViT-L + transformer)."
}

